d argumentative contribution, include vote. We can say we still support C but want to discuss A and D further. That would be okay.\n\nBut maybe better to challenge C's suitability due to communication and adaptability, and propose Candidate B as alternative because stress resilience and conscientiousness are critical. Let's see: B handles stress well, very conscientious (reliability), good at weather (technical), but makes nasty remarks (communication) and weak memory for numbers (attention). The nasty remarks are serious for crew cooperation. So both C and B have communication concerns.\n\nCandidate D: takes responsibility, solves problems, concentrates, responds adequately to unexpected events, but hot temper (stress) and know-it-all (cooperation). So also concerns.\n\nCandidate A: leadership, anticipation, but show-off, not open to new ideas, unorganized.\n\nAll have concerns. Which is best? Might be C because reliability and decision quality are top. So maybe we keep vote for C but ask for more info on communication and adaptability.\n\nThus our public message: agree with Agent 1's recommendation but highlight concerns, ask for more info on other candidates, maybe ask Agent 1 for more details on C's communication. But we need to include a vote. We'll vote Candidate C.\n\nLet's craft: \"I agree with Agent 1 that Candidate C's 100% reliability and quick correct decisions are critical, but I am concerned about communication difficulty and lack of willingness to further education, which could affect crew cooperation and adaptability. However, compared to the other candidates, whose reliability or stress resilience is less clear, C still appears strongest. Therefore I also vote for Candidate C, but suggest we probe further on his communication and adaptability, and also get more details on A, B, D.\"\n\nThat satisfies."}
